# The HOST crontab: ONE line per job. All locking, logging and evidence lines
# live inside the verb — the cron line carries no flock and no redirect, so it
# can never drift per host again. This is shared/crontab.example's charter,
# applied to the other side of the fleet, and it is not hypothetical there:
# five hand-edited variants of the on-box line drifted before that file was
# written, and a skipped tick that wrote nothing made a wedged bot
# indistinguishable from a healthy quiet one.
#
# THREE jobs are scheduled since 0.1.4: the host tick every five minutes, the
# daily restart, the weekly reset. Install with `crontab -e` on the box HOST
# (not in a box). $HOME is expanded by cron; the path is the link `install.sh`
# maintains.
#
# THE HOST NEEDS ITS OWN `gh` LOGIN. The tick reads every registered board
# with the host's gh, not a box's: `gh auth login` once, as the operator (or a
# read-only identity), before the first tick. A host whose gh is dead says so
# on every tick and neither wakes nor stops a box — blindness is never read as
# a quiet fleet. shared/docs/host-maintenance.md has the reading.
#
# Read the evidence in the host job log, not in cron mail:
#   ~/.local/state/crew/host-maintenance.log
# Every run writes a start line and an end line there, including a run that
# did nothing and a run that was skipped by the lock — so silence in that log
# at a boundary means exactly one thing: cron itself is dead.

# The host tick (#759): one board read per repository, boxes started only for
# work their member owns and stopped when idle, under FLEET_MAX_RUNNING. Five
# minutes, the engine's own cadence; the box's cron keeps ticking while it is
# up, and this line is what brings a stopped box back. A wake launches the
# box's tick in the background and returns, so one long session never holds
# this host's lock across the next boundaries.
*/5 * * * * $HOME/.local/bin/crew tick

# Daily — drain and cycle every roster box that is RUNNING. A busy box is
# SKIPPED by name and never forced; a box the host tick stopped stays stopped
# (the tick starts it when there is work), and a paused box is left paused.
# The run exits 3 when any box was skipped, and nothing is lost. It is also
# the fleet's routine liveness signal: grok-reviewer ran no session at all
# between 2026-08-19 and 2026-08-28, and nothing on the board or on the floor
# reported it.
10 4 * * * $HOME/.local/bin/crew restart --all

# The weekly reset (#761): every roster box back to its `armed` checkpoint,
# Sunday 05:10, its memory drained to the store first and written back onto
# the restored disk under the duty lock, so the first duty that runs reads the
# whole of it. What #678 deferred the reset for — duty.log and the ledgers
# lost with the disk — the store now holds; shared/docs/host-maintenance.md
# keeps that history and the ordering rule.
10 5 * * 0 $HOME/.local/bin/crew reset --all

# The real-host drill is operator-attended by construction and is
# deliberately not scheduled here.
